#N canvas 40 40 1400 820 12;
#X text 20 20 AP Probe -- run this ONCE while the access point is up and the phone has joined it. It answers the three questions that cannot be asked any other way \, writes them to /sdcard/ap-probe.log \, and puts the phone's address on the screen., f 58;
#X text 620 20 SELECTING THIS PATCH IS ITSELF THE TEST. Loading it restarts Pd \, so if create_ap is still alive when the probe runs \, the access point survived a patch change -- which is exactly what Start AP depends on and nothing has yet proved., f 58;
#X text 20 180 IT RECORDS ITSELF BECAUSE NOBODY CAN WATCH. A Mac joined to this access point has no internet \, so the session that would answer these questions is the session with no help in it. Run it \, rejoin the house wifi \, read the log afterwards. Same reasoning as u_err's persistent log., f 58;
#X obj 20 350 loadbang;
#X obj 20 400 t b b;
#X obj 20 470 del 1200;
#X msg 20 520 sh ap-probe.sh;
#X obj 20 570 shell;
#X obj 20 620 t a a;
#X obj 20 690 print PHONE;
#X obj 300 690 s screenLine3;
#X obj 620 350 t b b;
#X msg 620 420 symbol AP-PROBE-RAN;
#X obj 620 470 s screenLine1;
#X msg 940 420 symbol phone-address;
#X obj 940 470 s screenLine2;
#X text 20 740 EXACTLY ONE LINE REACHES STDOUT and it is the phone's address. Everything else the probe finds goes to the log \, so the shell outlet carries a single symbol and can go straight to the screen., f 58;
#X text 620 560 If line 3 reads none \, either the phone had not joined yet or the lease could not be parsed. THE LOG SAYS WHICH \, and telling those two apart is the whole reason for writing one., f 46;
#X connect 3 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 1 11 0;
#X connect 4 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 8 1 9 0;
#X connect 8 0 10 0;
#X connect 11 1 12 0;
#X connect 12 0 13 0;
#X connect 11 0 14 0;
#X connect 14 0 15 0;
